title: 10. Lambdas, it and function types
nav: Learn
order: 1000
template: docs
---
<p>A lambda is a small function written where you use it: another function takes it and calls it. This chapter counts the list numbers that pass a test, tries tests of every kind, and meets the function type, the shorthand <code>it</code> and the limits that keep lambdas cheap.</p>
<p><a href="reading.html">Reading Bayan code</a> shows <code>it</code> in one line; here are the rules. You need parameters and particles (<a href="book-04-functions.html">chapter 4</a>), spans (<a href="book-07-collections.html">chapter 7</a>) and error sets (<a href="book-08-errors.html">chapter 8</a>).</p>

<h2 id="types">A function type</h2>
<p>A function type says what goes in and comes out with an arrow: <code>i64 -&gt; bool</code> takes an <code>i64</code>, gives a <code>bool</code>. Several inputs go in parentheses, <code>(i64, i64) -&gt; i64</code>; no inputs is <code>() -&gt; void</code>. A <code>type</code> line names it; a parameter is written type first.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type Pred = i64 -&gt; bool

i64 count(ReadOnlySpan&lt;i64&gt; xs, by Pred check) {
    var n = 0
    for x in xs {
        if check(x) {
            n += 1
        }
    }
    return n
}</code></pre>
<p><code>count</code> walks the span and calls <code>check</code> on each element like any function. Without an alias, the type goes in parentheses before the name: <code>by(i64 -&gt; i64) f</code>.</p>

<h2 id="lambdas">Passing a lambda</h2>
<p>A lambda is a parameter name, an arrow and an expression: <code>x =&gt; x % 3 == 0</code>. Where a one-parameter function is expected, skip the name and write <code>it</code>; the compiler reads any argument mentioning <code>it</code> as a lambda whose parameter is called <code>it</code>.</p>
<example name="book-10-pred"/>
<p><code>it &gt; 3</code> passes 4, 5 and 6 (<code>3</code>); <code>x =&gt; x % 3 == 0</code> passes 3 and 6 (<code>2</code>); <code>isEven</code>, an ordinary function of the right shape, passes 2, 4 and 6 (<code>3</code>). The fourth line reads <code>limit</code>, a <code>let</code> from the enclosing function; only 5 and 6 are above 4 (<code>2</code>). The last stores a lambda in a typed local and calls it.</p>
<p>A lambda takes its parameter types and result from the expected function type, so it needs no annotations.</p>

<h3 id="shapes">More than one parameter, and block bodies</h3>
<p>Two or more parameters go in parentheses, and a body may be a block with its own <code>return</code>.</p>
<example name="book-10-apply"/>
<p><code>it * 2</code> on 5 gives <code>10</code>; <code>(a, b) =&gt; a * b</code> on 3 and 4 gives <code>12</code>; the block body doubles 5 and adds 1, giving <code>11</code>. A <code>return</code> inside a lambda leaves the lambda, not the function around it.</p>

<h2 id="named">Named functions as values</h2>
<p>A module-level function passes by name when its signature matches the expected function type exactly, as <code>isEven</code> does. Particles are not part of the type: <code>bool atLeast(i64 x, by i64 d)</code> is an <code>(i64, i64) -&gt; bool</code>. A wrong shape is refused, and the message shows both.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2202:</span> expected `i64 -&gt; i64`, found `i64 shifted(i64, i64)`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> l7.byn:4:27

<span class="gut">  4 │</span>     print($"{apply(5, by: shifted)}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>                           <span class="hl">^^^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> pass a function of type `i64 -&gt; i64`

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2202</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>Methods, constructors and generic functions cannot pass by name; wrap them in a lambda.</p>

<h2 id="it">The rules for <code>it</code></h2>
<p><code>it</code> exists only in an argument position expecting a one-parameter function; anywhere else it is an undefined name.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2001:</span> `it` is not defined
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> l10.byn:2:28

<span class="gut">  2 │</span>     let big: i64 -&gt; bool = it &gt; 3
<span class="gut">    │</span>                            <span class="hl">^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> did you mean `Io`?
<span class="gut">    │</span> 2 │     let big: i64 -&gt; bool = Io &gt; 3
<span class="gut">    │   │</span>                            <span class="add">++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2001</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>A lambda cannot use <code>it</code> inside another lambda that already uses it; name one of them.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2029:</span> `it` inside a lambda that already uses `it`; name one lambda
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> l8.byn:3:41

<span class="gut">  3 │</span>     print($"{apply(5, by: apply(it, by: it + 1))}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>                                         <span class="hl">^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> name the inner lambda's parameter: `x =&gt; …`

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2029</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>A leading dot is not a member of the parameter: <code>.active</code> means a member of the expected type, as in <code>return .notFound</code>; the compiler shows what you meant.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2823:</span> `.active` is a member, not a lambda
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> l6.byn:7:31

<span class="gut">  7 │</span>     print($"{any(us.span, by: .active)}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>                               <span class="hl">^^^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> write `it.active`
<span class="gut">    │</span> 7 │     print($"{any(us.span, by: it.active)}")
<span class="gut">    │   │</span>                               <span class="add">++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2823</span></pre>
</figure>

<h2 id="mismatch">When the lambda does not fit</h2>
<p>The lambda is checked against the function type it is given to:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Problem</th><th>Refused at</th></tr>
<tr><td>wrong result type</td><td>the body</td></tr>
<tr><td>wrong number of parameters</td><td>the lambda</td></tr>
<tr><td>no function type to learn from</td><td>nothing to take its types from</td></tr>
</table>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2202:</span> expected `bool`, found `i64`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> l11.byn:13:41

<span class="gut">  13 │</span>     print($"{sumWhere(xs.span, by: x =&gt; x + 1)}")
<span class="gut">     │</span>                                         <span class="hl">^^^^^</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2202</span></pre>
</figure>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2820:</span> this lambda takes 2 parameter(s) but `i64 -&gt; i64` takes 1
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> l3.byn:3:27

<span class="gut">  3 │</span>     print($"{apply(5, by: (a, b) =&gt; a + b)}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>                           <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> write 1 parameter name(s): `x =&gt; …`

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2820</span></pre>
</figure>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2218:</span> `x =&gt; x + 1` needs a known type here
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> l2.byn:2:13

<span class="gut">  2 │</span>     let f = x =&gt; x + 1
<span class="gut">    │</span>             <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> write the function type it has, such as `let f: i64 -&gt; i64 = x =&gt; x`

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2218</span></pre>
</figure>

<h2 id="captures">What a lambda can read</h2>
<p>A lambda may read a <code>let</code>, a constant, a loop variable or a parameter from around it; the value is copied when the lambda is made, which makes the fourth line of <code>book-10-pred</code> safe, since <code>limit</code> cannot change afterwards.</p>
<p>A <code>var</code> cannot be captured: a lambda that could see a variable change would make code hard to follow, so the compiler asks for a copy.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2821:</span> `total` cannot be captured: it is a `var`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> l4.byn:4:32

<span class="gut">  4 │</span>     print($"{apply(5, by: it + total)}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>                                <span class="hl">^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> copy it into a `let` first

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2821</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>The same rule covers <code>ref</code> parameters and <code>this</code>. To update a counter, write the loop out or pass it as a <code>ref</code> parameter to a function.</p>

<h2 id="storing">Where a function type may go</h2>
<p>A function type fits three places and no others:</p>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Places</th></tr>
<tr><td>Allowed</td><td>as a parameter type, as the target of a <code>type</code> alias, as the type of a local</td></tr>
<tr><td>Not allowed</td><td>as a field, a return type, a constant, a payload, or an element of a list or map</td></tr>
</table>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2822:</span> a function type is not supported as a field yet
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> l5.byn:3:5

<span class="gut">  3 │</span>     Pred check
<span class="gut">    │</span>     <span class="hl">^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> take the function as a parameter instead

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2822</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>The help line gives the cure: take the function as a parameter on each call. A lambda also borrows the function that made it, so it cannot outlive that function.</p>

<h2 id="errors">Lambdas and errors</h2>
<p>A function type can list its errors after the result: <code>string -&gt; i64 fails ParseError</code>. A lambda given that type may fail too, and its caller handles it as usual; a failing call inside a lambda still needs <code>try</code> or <code>catch</code>.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2602:</span> `number` can fail; handle the error or pass it on
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> l14.byn:20:42

<span class="gut">  20 │</span>     let t2 = parseAll(bad.span, by: s =&gt; number(s)) catch -1
<span class="gut">     │</span>                                          <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">     │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> propagate the error with `try`, or handle it with `catch`
<span class="gut">     │</span> 20 │     let t2 = parseAll(bad.span, by: s =&gt; try number(s)) catch -1
<span class="gut">     │    │</span>                                          <span class="add">++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2602</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>Add <code>try</code>, and the error flows out through the function type.</p>
<example name="book-10-fails"/>
<p>The first call passes <code>number</code> by name and sums 4 and 5, so <code>9</code>. The second list holds <code>"x"</code>, which fails, so <code>parseAll</code> fails and its caller falls back to <code>-1</code>; the first <code>catch 0</code> never runs.</p>

<h2 id="planned">What is planned</h2>
<note>Planned: not in the compiler yet. Collection methods that take a lambda (<code>where</code>, <code>select</code>, <code>sumBy</code>, <code>sort(by:)</code>) are specified; today, write the loop once as a function taking a function type, as <code>count</code> does. Unavailable too: function types with a type parameter (<code>type Predicate&lt;T&gt;</code>), typed lambda parameters, <code>move</code> lambdas, lambdas capturing a <code>var</code>, stored function values.</note>
<pre><code class="byn">let big = xs.where(it &gt; 3)
let doubled = xs.select(x =&gt; x * 2)
let total = lines.sumBy(it.price * it.qty)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2003:</span> `where` is not a member of `List&lt;i64&gt;`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> l12.byn:3:17

<span class="gut">  3 │</span>     let ys = xs.where(it &gt; 1)
<span class="gut">    │</span>                 <span class="hl">^^^^^</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2003</span></pre>
</figure>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<details class="quiz"><summary>What does the last <code>print</code> in <code>book-10-pred</code> output, and why?</summary>
<p><code>true false</code>: <code>small</code> is <code>x =&gt; x &lt; 3</code>, so <code>small(2)</code> is true and <code>small(7)</code> false. Not <code>false true</code>, and not an error: a lambda stores in a local with a declared function type.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>You write <code>let f = x =&gt; x + 1</code> with no type. What happens?</summary>
<p>BYN2218: a lambda takes its types from the expected function type, and a plain <code>let</code> gives none. Write <code>let f: i64 -&gt; i64 = x =&gt; x + 1</code>; it does not guess <code>i64</code> from the body.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>Inside <code>main</code> with <code>var total = 0</code>, passing <code>it + total</code> to <code>apply</code>: (a) compiles and reads the current total, (b) compiles and reads a copy, (c) refused.</summary>
<p>(c). A lambda can capture a <code>let</code>, but a <code>var</code> is BYN2821; take a copy with <code>let snapshot = total</code> and use that. (a) and (b) assume a <code>var</code> can be captured.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>In <code>book-10-apply</code>, the block-bodied lambda contains <code>return doubled + 1</code>. What does that <code>return</code> leave?</summary>
<p>Only the lambda. <code>main</code> prints <code>11</code>, the value the lambda gave back; had the <code>return</code> left <code>main</code>, nothing would print for that line.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>Can a struct have a field of type <code>i64 -&gt; bool</code>?</summary>
<p>No: BYN2822, because a function type may only be a parameter, a <code>type</code> alias target or a local; take the function as a parameter on each call.</p></details>

